# .NET/C# Best Practices

Follow modern .NET conventions when writing or changing C# code in this repo.

## Style & Structure

- Prefer C# 12+ / current LTS .NET features when the project targets them
- Use meaningful domain names; keep methods focused and cohesive
- Prefer interfaces for testable boundaries (`I`-prefix)
- Match existing project namespace and folder layout; do not invent a new layout unless the sample defines one

## Dependency Injection & Async

- Prefer constructor injection (primary constructors when already used in the project)
- Register services with the correct lifetime (Singleton, Scoped, Transient)
- Use `async`/`await` for I/O; return `Task` / `Task<T>`; avoid `.Result` / `.Wait()`
- Use `ConfigureAwait(false)` in library code when appropriate

## Safety & Quality

- Validate inputs; throw specific exceptions with clear messages
- Use structured logging (`Microsoft.Extensions.Logging`) with useful context
- Prefer strongly typed configuration (`IOptions<T>` / data annotations) over magic strings
- Use parameterized queries / safe data access; never log secrets
- Dispose `IDisposable` / `IAsyncDisposable` resources correctly

## Testing

- Follow AAA (Arrange, Act, Assert)
- Cover success and failure paths; mock dependencies via interfaces
- Match the test framework already used in the sample (do not force MSTest/xUnit/NUnit)
